<Project>

  <!--
    KTSU0001 wants every project using Span<T>/Memory<T> to reference System.Memory, and the 47
    projects here that target netstandard2.1 do. That reference cannot be added: on the SDK CI
    builds with, NuGet rejects it outright during solution restore — "This package is automatically
    available and does not need to be referenced explicitly. Remove the PackageReference item."
    (NU1510, where NoWarn metadata on the item does not reach it).

    The two rules cannot both be satisfied, and NuGet is the one describing reality: the framework
    supplies the package, so KTSU0001's premise no longer holds on this SDK. Scoped to
    netstandard2.1, the only target it fires on.

    This lives in Directory.Build.targets rather than Directory.Build.props because ktsu.Sdk assigns
    NoWarn outright, and props is imported before the SDK - an addition there is overwritten.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup Condition="'$(TargetFramework)' == 'netstandard2.1'">
    <NoWarn>$(NoWarn);KTSU0001</NoWarn>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    ktsu.Sdk turns on EnablePackageValidation and ApiCompatValidateAssemblies, but the baseline half of
    package validation only runs once a baseline version is named. Without one, nothing compares a pack
    against what is already on nuget.org - which is how v2.3.2 shipped a binary-breaking change to
    ICommandExecutor as a patch (issue #24). Every public surface here is default interface methods, so
    adding an optional parameter stays source-compatible, reviews clean, and is caught by nothing until a
    consumer that upgraded without recompiling throws MissingMethodException.

    2.5.2 is the newest version published for all 70 packages, including the distribution providers that
    first shipped in 2.5.0 - an older baseline does not exist for those and would fail to restore. Move it
    forward when a break is intended and accepted; leaving it behind is safe, because an older baseline only
    widens what is compared.

    ktsu.Sdk also sets EnableStrictModeForBaselineValidation, which makes the comparison symmetric: every
    API *added* since the baseline is reported as well. That is the right setting for a servicing branch
    that must stay API-identical, and the wrong one here, where minor releases add providers and members -
    it would fail the build on exactly the changes this repository exists to make. Turned off so the
    baseline reports what it is here for: members that disappeared.

    Both live in Directory.Build.targets rather than .props for the reason above: the SDK assigns
    EnableStrictModeForBaselineValidation outright, and props is imported before the SDK.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <PackageValidationBaselineVersion Condition="'$(PackageValidationBaselineVersion)' == ''">2.5.2</PackageValidationBaselineVersion>
    <EnableStrictModeForBaselineValidation>false</EnableStrictModeForBaselineValidation>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!-- See ApiCompatExcludeAttributes.txt for why the one attribute it lists is not compared. -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <ApiCompatExcludeAttributesFile Include="$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)ApiCompatExcludeAttributes.txt" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
